1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,960
 All right, everybody.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,600
 You've made it the final week of course content, where we're going to be talking about

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,760
 manufacturing with engineering composites. The good news is that this week is going to be pretty

4
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:17,040
 cruzy. Most of the manufacturing techniques for engineering composites are slight variations on

5
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:21,520
 manufacturing techniques that we've already met one way or another this semester. But we are

6
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:25,440
 going to meet some specialist manufacturing techniques for specific types of composites,

7
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:30,080
 namely fiber reinforced polymers. From the outset, I'll note that manufacturing and

8
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:35,280
 manufacturing ability is actually often the impetus for choice of a composite engineering material

9
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:39,200
 in the first place. In addition to the superior properties that you can sometimes get out of a

10
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:44,240
 composite, they're also sometimes easier to manufacture than any of the constituent parts into a

11
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:48,640
 particular geometry. And just as a word of warning before we start, I should probably note that when

12
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:53,040
 you're manufacturing composites, perhaps more so than when you're manufacturing any other type of

13
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:57,440
 material, you need to be very careful. Remember when we're dealing with composites, we're stacking

14
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:02,240
 intrinsic assumptions and models and simplifications that we make about the constituent materials

15
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:06,240
 on top of assumptions and models and simplifications that we make about the way that these things

16
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:10,560
 are going to interact. And then when we talk about manufacturing of composites, we stack both

17
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:14,560
 of these assumptions on top of some assumptions and simplifications about the way that these

18
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:19,200
 properties are changed by the way we manufacture them, by the way they respond to a heat input

19
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,120
 or a plastic deformation, for example. When you kind of convoluted the stack up of the

20
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:27,360
 assumptions made for the constituent materials and the assumptions made about the way they interact

21
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:32,080
 and the assumptions made about the way that manufacturing changes these again, the properties of the

22
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:37,200
 part in service can vary significantly from what you expect. As always, if you want to be more sure

23
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:42,240
 about the properties of an in-service part, your best bet is to manufacture a prototype and test

24
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:44,240
 it for yourself.
